::ENG1_U01_L05_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The library added a return bin outside the entrance. Students could return books after school hours. Question: Which response is written correctly?{
~A. the return bin helped students return books after hours#Incorrect. This sentence needs a capital letter at the beginning and punctuation at the end.
~B. Because students could return books after hours.#Incorrect. This is a fragment because it begins with because and does not complete the thought.
=C. The return bin helped students return books after hours.#Correct. This is a complete sentence with correct capitalization and punctuation.
~D. The return bin helped students it was outside.#Incorrect. This is a run-on because two complete ideas are joined incorrectly.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The coach posted the practice plan before warm-ups. Players knew which station to visit first. Question: Which sentence fixes the run-on?{
~A. The plan helped players they knew which station to visit first.#Incorrect. This is still a run-on.
~B. Because players knew which station to visit first.#Incorrect. This is a fragment because it does not complete the thought.
=C. The plan helped players because they knew which station to visit first.#Correct. This connects the idea and the reason in one complete sentence.
~D. the plan helped players know the station#Incorrect. This needs capitalization, punctuation, and clearer wording.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The school posted bus updates on a screen near the office. Students checked the screen before leaving. Question: Which response is a complete sentence?{
=A. The screen helped students check bus updates before leaving.#Correct. This is a complete sentence with a subject, verb, and complete thought.
~B. Before leaving school.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~C. Because the screen had bus updates.#Incorrect. This is a fragment because the thought is incomplete.
~D. bus updates near the office#Incorrect. This is not a complete sentence and lacks capitalization and punctuation.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The cafeteria labeled vegetarian meals on the menu. Students could identify those choices quickly. Question: Which sentence uses capitalization and punctuation correctly?{
~A. the menu labels helped students identify vegetarian meals.#Incorrect. The first word should begin with a capital letter.
=B. The menu labels helped students identify vegetarian meals.#Correct. This sentence has correct capitalization and end punctuation.
~C. The menu labels helped students identify vegetarian meals#Incorrect. This sentence needs end punctuation.
~D. the menu labels helped students identify vegetarian meals#Incorrect. This sentence needs capitalization and end punctuation.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The teacher gave students a checklist before they submitted paragraphs. The checklist reminded them to check evidence and punctuation. Question: Which revision is best?{
~A. The checklist helped students revise paragraphs, it reminded them to check evidence and punctuation.#Incorrect. This comma creates a run-on sentence.
~B. Because it reminded them to check evidence and punctuation.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~C. the checklist helped students revise paragraphs#Incorrect. This needs capitalization, punctuation, and more complete explanation.
=D. The checklist helped students revise paragraphs because it reminded them to check evidence and punctuation.#Correct. This is a complete sentence that clearly connects the idea and reason.
}
